00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:17.710
On a cold and stormy morning in January 1953,
the Princess Victoria ferry was preparing

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:23.119
to leave its dock in Stranraer on the south-west
coast of Scotland, despite gale warnings.

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:27.099
An hour into its journey its captain radioed
for help as the storm forced the ferry on

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:30.340
its side, making it impossible to board the
lifeboats.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:36.050
Of the 176 people aboard the princess victoria,
only 43 survived, but there was more tragedy

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.190
to come, this storm was headed south towards
the Netherlands, pushing the seas with it,

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.300
and with the Moon and Sun causing even higher
tides, this storm would severely test the

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.360
flood defences of the Netherlands, which was
still getting back on its feet after the Second

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.360
World War.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.340
The storm would ultimately claim the lives
of 1,835 people in the Netherlands, along

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:59.199
with 200,000 cattle and flooding 2,000 square
kilometres of land, destroying 43,000 homes

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:01.350
forcing and 72,000 people to flee.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.060
Today, we are going to learn why this happened
and how it would spark the construction of

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:09.370
one of the seven modern wonders of the world:
The Dutch Delta Works.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:13.400
After World War 2, the meandering levees on
the coast of the Netherlands had fallen into

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.700
disrepair, the Netherlands were just getting
back onto their feet after 5 years German

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.620
occupation just 8 years prior to the storm.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.130
The poorly maintained flood defences were
a disaster waiting to happen.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.470
As the storm approached, it forced water inlands
with no-where to go, but up.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:33.130
This put intense pressure on the dykes and
levees of the low countries, and by the storm’s

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:38.590
end 139 kilometres of levees would be heavily
damaged, with holes up to 3.5 kilometres being

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:39.590
torn open.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.890
With nearly 26% of the Netherlands land area
being under sea level, seawater burst through

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.270
these breeches with immense strength causing
damage that would take decades to repair and

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:52.540
would spur the formation of the Delta Committee
just 20 days later to ensure this could never

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.340
happen again and this is what they came up
with.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.540
The new Delta plan would shorten the Dutch
coastline by 700 kilometres, by closing the

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.110
primary inlets in these 4 locations, this
would drastically reduce the length of levees

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.030
and dykes that needed to be inspected and
maintained and thus decreasing the chances

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.050
of weak points jeopardising the safety of
the Dutch people.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.590
However this was no easy task and would come
with an enormous cost.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:18.920
Before these works could be completed, additional
barriers needed to be erected upstream to

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.140
improve fresh and saltwater management, and
prevent fresh water emptying from the Rhine,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.970
Meuse and Schelde river from redirecting around
these new dams.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.940
The northern most closure dam also needed
to be equipped with a hydraulic sluice capable

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.140
of dealing with the output of the Rhine river,
as this Fresh water would flood the Netherlands

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.430
from the other direction, if it was prevented
from emptying into the North Sea.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:43.890
On top of all this, a number of ports, such
as the port of Rotterdam and the port of Antwerp,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:44.890
had to stay accessible.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:49.980
So, aside from fixed dams, bridges and sluices,
two new locks that would allow an inland ship

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.030
route between Antwerp and Rotterdam needed
to be built.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.010
Amazingly, on top of all this work, the dutch
still managed to consider the environmental

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.500
impact of this work.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.180
The largest of the structures built for the
Delta Works project, the Oosterschelde Storm

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.570
Surge Barrier, was originally planned to completely
close the mouth of this river, which would

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.460
create a fresh water basin.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.010
However resistance to this plan arose, as
it would completely change the saltwater environment

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.380
of the area.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.370
The Oosterschelde scenery is unique, with
a great variety of fish, water plants and

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.370
animals.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:24.120
So in 1976 the Dutch government agreed to
a different plan: Building an open barrier

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.940
that could be closed during heavy storms and
high tides.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.660
adding another 2.5 billion euros to the cost
to the project.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.780
This barrier is 9 kilometres long with 62
openings, each 40 metres wide, allowing the

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.770
tidal movement to remain in tact.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.220
To build a structure this massive, that needs
to not only support it’s own weight, but

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:46.160
the enormous force of a storm surge pushing
against it, would require extensive foundations.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.220
The first part of these foundations were created
by forming two islands, the biggest of which

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.560
housing a lock to allowing ships to pass through
the barrier.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:58.310
This island even included a massive dry dock
to construct the 65 pylons needed to support

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:04.120
the sluice gates, each using 7,000 cubic metres
of concrete and taking one-and-a-half years

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.180
to build.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.050
Between each island a trench was dug.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.590
On both sides of the trench, mats were placed
to keep the seabed in place.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:14.010
While specially built ships were then used
to consolidate the sand at the bottom of the

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.200
trench, using special vibrating needles, the
sand would be vibrated to pack the sand firmly

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.010
together creating a surface that could carry
the weight of the massive pylons.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:25.630
The trench itself was then covered with specially
made mats filled with rocks to help prevent

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.710
erosion of the underlying sand.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.440
The pylons were left hollow so they could
be picked up by another purpose-built, u-shaped

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.110
ship, and moved into place.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:37.710
There, inside the trench, on top of the mats,
they would be lowered, filled with sand and

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.090
closed with concrete.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:43.550
The wide foot of each pylon was packed in
stone, as it is vital the pylons never move,

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:48.750
because if even one of the massive, 260 to
480 tonne doors, cannot move, the current

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:52.710
in that location could become enormous and
potentially damage the structure.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.830
Finally these enormous hydraulic pistons were
attached to the sluice gates, allowing 3 kilometres

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:01.530
of the 9 kilometre long Storm Surge Barrier
to open and close on demand.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.880
This project truly is one of the modern wonders
of the world.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.710
Allowing the dutch to rule the tide and ensure
the chances of another devastating flood are

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:13.220
dramatically reduced, but with sea levels
continuing to rise and warmers seas causing

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:17.240
even stronger storms, we need to remind ourselves
of the lessons learned here.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:22.460
The flooding of New Orleans in 2005 occurred
for many of the same reasons as the 1953 flooding

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.740
of the Netherlands.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.949
Poorly maintained levees broke well below
their design tolerances, allowing 80% of the

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:30.500
city to be flooded in just 5 hours.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.280
Had New Orleans taken lessons from history
and reduced the length of defences needed,

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.711
as they have done now with the 1.1 billion
dollar Lake Borgne Surge Barrier, they may

00:05:38.711 --> 00:05:43.301
have saved of over 1000 people and prevented
the 108 billion dollars of damage the storm

00:05:43.301 --> 00:05:44.301
caused.

00:05:44.301 --> 00:05:47.900
If these trends continue cities around the
world are going to have to seriously assess

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.960
the risk of flooding and make plans to prevent
any chance of a flood taking the lives of

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.480
their citizens.

